A||||6||86SE||How do I make an engine safely rev higher?||||5||
Z||000000||86SE||08-23-2000||08:47 PM||||In the next month I will buy a 3.4 from TaCreations.com.  I have heard that the engines only rev to 5500, can anyone confirm this?<P>What kind of pushrods, lifters and rockers would I need to get.  This is a stock engine with about 50,000 miles from a 93-95 Camaro/Firebird.<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||5
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||08-23-2000||10:27 PM||||How high do you want to rev it? a balance job would be the best place to start, and is only $150 (when tacked onto a rebuild) after that, I'd say lighter pistons (the hyper whatchamacallit glass ones) Knife edging the crank (from what I hear this is EXPENCIVE!) roller hydralic lifters (PITA, and not recomended for road use) roller rockers, guide plates... It gets very expencive. If you have the cash, take it to the shop and say "Make it bullet proof!"||||reg||1
Z||000002||olympic||08-23-2000||11:33 PM||||  I can thing of a few:<BR>-forged rods, pistons, crank<BR>-premium fasteners (ARP)<BR>-windage tray<BR>-stronger valve springs<BR>-roller rockers and screw in studs<BR>-stronger pushrods<BR>-have everything align bored and honed<BR>-balance the whole rotating assembly<BR>-and a good port/polish/header/intake to gain flow for high rpm power.||||reg||3
Z||000003||Xtreme Fiero's||08-24-2000||12:50 AM||||Use Foraged everything, Roller rockers and get real good Dual valve springs. Also Unless you open up the intake track with Cold air intake larger Throttle body, ported heads & intake you will rev pelnty high but you won't make any power.<P>   You know my view on Hyper pistons, they are light and would rev high but I stand by the fact that they won't hold up to the abuse of NO2.<P>    Just open up the intake and beef up the Valve train, That should bump up the red line but i don't know how much, 500-1500 RPM? That would give you a 6500 RPM redline.||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||08-24-2000||08:20 AM||||like the others said.. upgrade the valve springs and valves to lighter and stiffer. and make sure you have a dyno to run the car on.. i could tach my 2.8 to 7200 on the dyno but it wasn't making more hp or more torque just sucking wind and wasting gas... ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wannabe||08-24-2000||10:39 AM||||With the same heads as the 2.8, the 3.4 is going to be much less rev-happy. You are trying to pull 20% more air through holes the same size. Just the same, the first place to go is the camshaft and stronger valve springs. You might also want to consider having the heads ported with a 3 or 5 angle valve job. I don't know if valves with undercut stems are available for this engine, but they would also help a lot. Whatevery you do, within the operating limits of the stock ecm, you aren't likely to get much useful power beyond 6000rpm.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||08-24-2000||10:57 AM||||Have you checked with holley. They have complete kits for allot of motors. So do other aftermarket operations. These are always a better choice for people that have little experience buying individual items. Even many pro's use the kits because it gives a predictable result. They may fool with it more later or not depending on what they want but at least they have a good sound base to work with. Buying assorted parts from diferant sources is a good way to end up with a giant headache.||||reg||1
